WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Speaker 1: I want to start today with a story that&nbsp;
several of you sent me about a black democratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.360
leader who threatened, who was threatened&nbsp;
with lynching for criticizing the former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.720
President Donald Trump. Let's go right to the&nbsp;
New York Times article. Iowa Democratic chair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:22.160
says he received lynching threat after criticizing&nbsp;
Trump Ross Wilburn, the first black person to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.520
lead Iowa's Democratic Party, said he received&nbsp;
several racist messages after writing a column&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.800
in the Des Moines Register. Iowa's&nbsp;
Democratic Party chair said on Tuesday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.920
he received a lynching threat and several other&nbsp;
racist phone and email messages after he wrote a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.800
column in the state's largest newspaper denouncing&nbsp;
former President Donald Trump and Republicans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.200
The party chairman Ross Wilburn, the first black&nbsp;
person to lead the Democratic Party in Iowa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.680
the presidential proving ground, said that he&nbsp;
turned the messages over to the police in Ames,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:57.040
Iowa, and planned to press charges if the&nbsp;
people who sent the messages were identified.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.520
Speaking to reporters over Zoom, Mr. Wilburn,&nbsp;
a state representative from Ames, said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.160
the threatening messages were in response to an&nbsp;
October 8th opinion article that he wrote in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:12.160
Des Moines Register titled Iowa Republicans Put&nbsp;
Loyalty to Trump over helping Iowans. The columns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:18.480
publication preceded Trump's rally in Iowa, his&nbsp;
first visit since losing in 2020 and since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.120
Jan. six insurrection, Wilburn said Tuesday.&nbsp;
At some point, we have to say enough is enough.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.520
We can't control thoughts that people filled with&nbsp;
hate have. But when you speak out, when you choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:33.200
to act in certain ways, that's when it's not&nbsp;
OK. On October 8th, Wilburn said he received a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.080
voicemail from a restricted number, ending with&nbsp;
a threat that included a reference to lynching.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.560
Over the next two days, he said, racial slurs were&nbsp;
repeatedly directed at him. In another voicemail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:48.080
and an email sent to his legislative account,&nbsp;
Commander Jason Tuttle of Ames Police Department&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.920
said in an email that Wilburn filed the police&nbsp;
report and police were investigating. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:02:00.800
one of the things. There's so many layers to the&nbsp;
the speech rights that the right likes to claim.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:07.120
It's not shocking that the pro-Trump people, when&nbsp;
they got angry with a black guy for criticizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:12.240
Trump, they said racist things. To him, that's&nbsp;
not a shock. That's kind of like blasé in a sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.960
sadly. But but it is at this point we've become&nbsp;
very, very used to it. You know, they really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:24.960
have this confounding, convoluted understanding&nbsp;
of speech when it comes to, as we talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:34.160
yesterday, the right being angry that stories they&nbsp;
want to see in the mainstream don't get enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:40.240
attention because they're dumb stories. They say&nbsp;
that it's suppression of speech. Now, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:45.867
media outlets and people can choose, I want to&nbsp;
talk about this or I don't want to talk about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.867 --> 00:02:50.800
is not necessarily an indication of suppression&nbsp;
of speech or censorship or self-censorship. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:59.040
stories are just kind of dumb. Then when there&nbsp;
are consequences to their speech, they say, Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:04.240
that's a restriction of my freedom of speech.&nbsp;
No, you were allowed to speak and say what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:09.840
wanted about the election results or about lab&nbsp;
leak or Hunter Biden or whatever. But also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:16.480
there are reactions to your speech by people who&nbsp;
themselves have the right to speak as well, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:23.840
they don't like that, that that's not good. But&nbsp;
then here, instead of this just being a reaction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:29.760
a consequence to the speech of Mr. Wilburn, they&nbsp;
actually go over the line because threatening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:37.040
people with a lynching or with personal injury or&nbsp;
calling them and leaving repeated racial slurs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.480
Those may be the consequences to Wilburn speech,&nbsp;
but that goes beyond into criminality because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:47.520
those are now threats and it's harassment.&nbsp;
So like on every level on this speech thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.280
enforcing terms of service is not a First&nbsp;
Amendment violation. It's not even the government.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.320
It's a private company enforcing their terms of&nbsp;
service reaction to the thing I said. Well, now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:04.880
my speech is being suppressed by that speech. No,&nbsp;
it's just the consequence of your speech. And as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:09.120
long as it's not illegal, threatening or otherwise&nbsp;
against the law, it's just something you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.880
deal with when you speak. People might react to&nbsp;
that. OK, well, then I'm going to react to what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:18.480
Wilburn said by threatening with a lynching. No,&nbsp;
that actually is a crime. At this point in time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:26.080
It's a continued and persistent misunderstanding&nbsp;
of every aspect of what free speech, censorship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.320
and the ability to say what you want to say in&nbsp;
media outlets to cover what they want to cover.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.320
They misunderstand all of it at every&nbsp;
single level, obviously, in the case of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:40.400
Mr. Wilburn. Let's hope they actually find the&nbsp;
people who made those horrible racist threats.

